  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Dryad dataset is still early in its visible use: it has 4 recorded downloads and no citations, scite links, or public mentions in the available record. The clearest sign of reach is a direct citation connection to a related Science article, which suggests the dataset is being positioned as supporting evidence for published research.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dataset behind <em>Home alone: Remote work, isolation, and mental health</em> shows modest but concrete early uptake. Usage logs record 4 downloads and no page views, while OpenAlex currently shows 0 citations. In other words, the record points to very limited circulation so far, but not to absence of relevance: the work is newly indexed and the usage evidence that does exist is at least traceable.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal is bibliographic rather than attention-based. Crossref data citations indicate that the dataset is cited by a Science article, showing that it has been incorporated into formal scholarly work. That is an important marker for a dataset, because it reflects reuse or acknowledgement within the research literature rather than casual interest.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding discovery signals are otherwise sparse. Scite reports no supporting or contradicting citations, and the event-data record shows no captured online mentions. Those gaps should be read cautiously: they indicate that the current archive does not surface broader public discussion, not that the dataset has had none.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The content and authorship also point to a study situated at the intersection of labor economics, mental health, and pandemic-era workplace change, with contributions from Natalia Emanuel, Amanda Pallais, and Emma Harrington. Overall, the dataset\u2019s footprint is currently limited but credible: a small amount of usage, no broad attention yet, and one clear sign of scholarly reuse through citation in a major journal article.</p>",
